**Read:** [Production of Large Volume Organic Chemicals, BREF LVOC](../sources/Large-Volume-Organic-Chemicals-BREF.lingua#r_78ET6A4D7AX8HPQTYAMZMAG16B), the complete ethylene-oxide and ethylene-glycol chapter; [Organic Chemical Technology — Nanda Kishore, NPTEL](../sources/Organic-Chemical-Technology-Kishore.lingua#r_1ERYESBGNKCKMDY0NPHJKCVJG0), the entire relevant olefin oxidation/derivative sequence; [Designing and operating safe chemical reaction processes — HSE HSG143](../sources/Chemical-Reaction-Processes-HSE-HSG143.lingua#r_4W5NNM3T7AY4AWFT30RZRJQAST), the full applicable reaction development and thermal/scale assessment methods.

The desired oxidation C₂H₄+½O₂→C₂H₄O competes with other transformations, including more complete oxidation. Catalyst state, local composition, heat/transport and residence history affect product distribution. A high ethylene conversion does not automatically imply high useful oxide yield.

Subsequent oxide-water chemistry forms glycol products with its own selectivity and separation questions. Water amount, competing/continued reaction, product distribution and removal influence isolated grades. Recycled carriers, unreacted feed and trace species connect reactor and separation sections.

Reconstruct the complete product conditioning, gas/liquid duties, heat recovery, wastewater and residual account. Chemical reactivity and thermal behavior require the actual primary methods and appropriate professional setting; a simplified equation is a teaching relation, not operating knowledge for reactive equipment.

**Work:** reconstruct the entire public oxide/glycol process chapter with complete primary chemistry, phase and performance evidence. Independently close carbon, oxygen, water and energy, compare a documented process or catalyst change at equal accepted products, and explain the observations needed to distinguish reaction selectivity, recovery and analytical effects.

